Q7: link the system libdbus, never a vendored copy

On aarch64 Linux the manifest adds `dbus` with the `vendored` feature. The
library itself never uses `dbus`; the line is there so that feature
unification turns on `libdbus-sys/vendored` for the BLE stack (bluer),
which then compiles libdbus from source and links it statically into the
binary on the one architecture Sukkula ships for.

A static libdbus is a second D-Bus implementation in the process, next to
the system's libdbus-1.so.3 that everything else (Qt, Sailfish's own
libraries, Sukkula's Bluetooth code) uses, and one that the operating
system's security updates never reach. Jolla's Harbour rules allow
linking the system libdbus-1.so.3, and that is what the `dbus` crate
does by default (through pkg-config).

The dependency goes. Sukkula's own BLE nudge (F-QS2) uses the `dbus`
crate over the system libdbus, and its cargo-deny policy refuses the
`vendored` feature of `dbus` and `libdbus-sys` from any crate.

Findings: Q7 (spec §5). Report: docs/UPSTREAM-QUICKSHARE.md Q7.
